      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T14:03:05.655942",
      "agent_id": "pro_agent",
      "round": 1,
      "text": "The consumption of red meat increases the risk of colorectal cancer. Our position is supported by the World Health Organization's classification of processed meat as a carcinogen. A key study found that consuming 50 grams of processed meat daily increases the risk of colorectal cancer by 18%.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "title": "IARC Monographs Volume 114",
          "url": "https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/IARC_Monographs_Volume114.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T14:03:06.266899",
      "agent_id": "con_agent",
      "round": 1,
      "text": "The World Health Organization's classification of processed meat as a carcinogen does not necessarily imply that all red meat increases the risk of colorectal cancer, as the organization specifically noted that unprocessed red meat was classified as 'probably carcinogenic to humans'.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "title": "Q2 2015 IARC Monograph Classification of the carcinogenicity of the consumption of red meat and processed meat",
          "url": "https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/Press-Release-230.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T14:03:06.736661",
      "agent_id": "pro_agent",
      "round": 2,
      "text": "The World Health Organization's classification of processed meat as a carcinogen does imply a link between red meat consumption and colorectal cancer risk, as the organization's classification is based on the cumulative evidence from multiple studies.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "title": "Q2 2015 IARC Monograph on the Evaluation of Carcinogenic Risks to Humans",
          "url": "https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/mono153-EN.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T14:03:07.129356",
      "agent_id": "con_agent",
      "round": 2,
      "text": "The World Health Organization's classification of processed meat as a carcinogen does not directly imply a link between all red meat consumption and colorectal cancer risk, as the organization's classification is based on the specific characteristics of processed meat, not unprocessed red meat.",
      "citations": [
