    },
    {
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T14:04:07.087312",
      "agent_id": "pro_agent",
      "round": 4,
      "text": "The organization's classification of processed meat as a carcinogen does indeed imply a link between red meat consumption and colorectal cancer risk, as the underlying mechanisms driving carcinogenicity in processed meat are also present in unprocessed red meat.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "title": "Carcinogenicity of processed meat.",
          "url": "https://www.iarc.fr/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/webpdfs/IARC_Monograph_114.pdf"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T14:04:07.534330",
      "agent_id": "con_agent",
      "round": 4,
      "text": "The organization's classification of processed meat as a carcinogen does not imply that the underlying mechanisms driving carcinogenicity in processed meat are also present in unprocessed red meat, as the carcinogenic effects of processed meat are largely attributed to its high levels of advanced glycation end (AGE) products.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "title": "Advanced glycation end products in food and a possible link to human health",
          "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4444166/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "timestamp": "2026-05-30T14:05:05.933999",
      "agent_id": "pro_agent",
      "round": 5,
      "text": "The opponent's claim that AGE products are the primary drivers of carcinogenicity in processed meat is an oversimplification, as other factors such as heme iron and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons also contribute to the increased risk of colorectal cancer.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "title": "Advanced glycation end products and colorectal cancer risk",
